During a recent network upgrade where we're seeing frequent route flaps in certain prefixes, I need to implement BGP dampening on our Nokia 7750 SR routers to mitigate the propagation of these unstable routes—but I want to ensure this doesn't introduce unnecessary delays for well-behaved paths that should converge normally. What's the best approach to tune the suppression thresholds and half-life parameters specifically for this hardware platform?

Retrieved Reference Content (from the configuration manual):  
{
    "PageTitle": "sr-policy",
    "FuncDef": "This command enables the use of segment routing policies to resolve the BGP next hop of certain BGP routes (depending on the context). The segment routing policies that are considered are statically configured in the local router or learned by BGP routes (AFI 1/SAFI 73). For a BGP route to be resolved by an SR policy, the highest numbered color extended community attached to BGP route must match the color of the SR policy. Next hop resolution of VPN IP routes by SR policies is not supported.",
    "CLIs": [
        "sr-policy",
        "no sr-policy"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>next-hop-resolution>shortcut-tunn>family>resolution-filter",
        "config>router>bgp>next-hop-resolution>labeled-routes>transport-tunnel>family>resolution-filter"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": ""
}
{
    "PageTitle": "ignore-router-id",
    "FuncDef": "When theignore-router-idcommand is present, and the current best path to a destination was learned from EBGP peer X with BGP identifier x and a new path is received from EBGP peer Y with BGP identifier y, the best path remains unchanged if the new path is equivalent to the current best path up to the BGP identifier comparison - even if y is less than x.",
    "CLIs": [
        "ignore-router-id include-internal <family> [ <family> ]",
        "[ no ] ignore-router-id"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>best-path-selection",
        "config>service>vprn>bgp>best-path-selection"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "family",
            "Info": "Specifies up to two internal families to be included in this configuration. Values-mvpn-ipv4, mvpn-ipv6."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no ignore-router-id"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "loop-detect",
    "FuncDef": "This command configures how the BGP peer session handles loop detection in the AS path.",
    "CLIs": [
        "loop-detect { drop-peer | discard-route | ignore-loop | off }",
        "no loop-detect"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "drop-peer",
            "Info": "Sends a notification to the remote peer and drops the session."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "discard-route",
            "Info": "Discards routes received from a peer with the same AS number as the router itself. This option prevents routes looped back to the router from being added to the routing information base and consuming memory. When this option is changed, the change will not be active for an established peer until the connection is re-established for the peer."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "ignore-loop",
            "Info": "Ignores routes with loops in the AS path but maintains peering."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "off",
            "Info": "Disables loop detection."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: loop-detect ignore-loop"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "damping",
    "FuncDef": "This command enables BGP route damping for learned routes which are defined within the route policy. Use damping to reduce the number of update messages sent between BGP peers and reduce the load on peers without affecting the route convergence time for stable routes. Damping parameters are set via route policy definition.",
    "CLIs": [
        "damping",
        "no damping"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no damping"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "prefix-limit",
    "FuncDef": "This command configures the maximum number of BGP routes that can be received from a peer before some administrative action is taken. The administrative action can be the generation of a log event or taking down the session. If a session is taken down, then it can be brought back up automatically after an idle-timeout period, or else it can be configured to stay down ('forever') until the operator performs a reset.",
    "CLIs": [
        "prefix-limit <family> <limit> [ threshold <percentage> ] [ idle-timeout { <minutes> | forever } | log-only ] [ post-import ]",
        "no prefix-limit <family>"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "log-only",
            "Info": "Enables the warning message to be sent at the specified threshold percentage, and also when the limit is reached. However, the BGP session is not taken down."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "percentage",
            "Info": "Specifies the threshold value (as a percentage) that triggers a warning message to be sent. Values-1 to 100."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "family",
            "Info": "Specifies the address family to which the limit applies. Values-ipv4, label-ipv4, vpn-ipv4, ipv6, label-ipv6, vpn-ipv6, mcast-ipv4, l2-vpn, mvpn-ipv4, mdt-safi, ms-pw, flow-ipv4, route-target, mcast-vpn-ipv4, mvpn-ipv6, flow-ipv6, evpn, mcast-ipv6, bgp-ls."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "limit",
            "Info": "Specifies the number of routes that can be learned from a peer expressed as a decimal integer. Values-1 to 4294967295."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "minutes",
            "Info": "Specifies duration in minutes before automatically re-establishing a session. Values-1 to 1024."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "forever",
            "Info": "Specifies that the session is reestablished only after clear router bgp command is executed."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "post-import",
            "Info": "Specifies that the limit should be applied only to the number of routes that are accepted by import policies."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": ""
}
{
    "PageTitle": "graceful-restart",
    "FuncDef": "This command enables BGP graceful restart helper procedures (the \"receiving router\" role defined in the standard) for address families included in the GR capabilities of both peers. SR OS can support GR helper functionality for IPv4, IPv6, VPN-IPv4, VPN-IPv6, Label-IPv4, Label-IPv6, L2-VPN, Route-Target (RTC), Flow-IPv4 (IPv4 flow-spec) and Flow-IPv6 (IPv6 flow-spec) routes.",
    "CLIs": [
        "graceful-restart",
        "no graceful-restart"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no graceful-restart"
}

