## User question:
"During a recent core network upgrade, we need to ensure our Nokia 7750 SR routers selectively advertise a default route (0.0.0.0/0) only to certain downstream peers via BGP, rather than propagating it throughout the entire AS. What would be the most efficient method to implement this selective default route origination in the BGP configuration while maintaining strict control over route distribution?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To selectively advertise the default route (0.0.0.0/0) only to specific downstream peers via BGP on the Nokia 7750 SR, use the following CLI configuration commands:

```cli
/configure router policy-options
begin
prefix-list "DEFAULT_ROUTE"
    prefix 0.0.0.0/0 exact
exit
policy-statement "ADVERTISE_DEFAULT"
    entry 10
        from
            prefix-list "DEFAULT_ROUTE"
        exit
        to
            neighbor <downstream-peer-IP>
        exit
        action accept
    exit
    default-action reject
exit
commit
exit

/configure router bgp group "DOWNSTREAM_PEERS"
    export "ADVERTISE_DEFAULT"
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
1. **`prefix-list "DEFAULT_ROUTE`"** matches the exact default route (0.0.0.0/0).  
2. **`policy-statement "ADVERTISE_DEFAULT`"** explicitly advertises this prefix only to specified downstream peers (replace `<downstream-peer-IP>` with the actual peer IP(s)).  
3. **`export "ADVERTISE_DEFAULT"`** applied to the BGP group "DOWNSTREAM_PEERS" ensures only peers in this group receive the default route. Peers outside this group will not receive it.  

This method efficiently restricts default route propagation to designated peers while preventing advertisement throughout the entire AS. Replace `"DOWNSTREAM_PEERS"` and `<downstream-peer-IP>` with your specific group name and peer IPs.
