## User question:
During a recent core router upgrade project, we're encountering path MTU discovery issues where TCP sessions aren't properly adjusting packet sizes despite receiving ICMP fragmentation-needed messages—what's the recommended method to configure our Nokia 7750 SR platforms to dynamically adapt MSS values based on ICMP feedback to optimize throughput without manual intervention?
---
## Proposed answer:
To resolve path MTU discovery issues and enable dynamic MSS adjustment for TCP sessions on your Nokia 7750 SR, enable path MTU discovery in the BGP configuration:

```
configure router bgp
    path-mtu-discovery
exit
```

This configures the router to:
1. Set the initial MTU for BGP TCP sessions to the egress interface MTU
2. Enable the DF (Don't Fragment) bit in IP headers
3. Dynamically adjust MTU/MSS based on received ICMP fragmentation-needed messages
4. Optimize throughput automatically without requiring manual intervention

The configuration applies globally to all BGP sessions. For neighbor-specific adjustments, apply under `group` or `neighbor` contexts as needed.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
path-mtu-discovery
no path-mtu-discovery
FuncDef:
This command enables path MTU discovery for the associated TCP connections. In doing so, the MTU for the associated TCP session will be initially set to the egress interface MTU. The DF bit will also be set so that if a router along the path of the TCP connection cannot handle a packet of a particular size without fragmenting, it will send back and ICMP message to set the path MTU for the given session to a lower value that can be forwarded without fragmenting.
ParaDef:
No ParaDef found
